# Every fusion's converted parts, summed per quantity. One row per (composed layer, quantity)
# composition/converted.sqlc has a part for: the three points summed, and how many parts were.
#
# The parts into their fusion, weighted
#   composition/converted.sqlc is one row per part and quantity with the part's figure in the
#   composed layer's unit. Summed per fusion and quantity it is `F Φ x` for that quantity, the side
#   of a fusion's sum that comes from its parts. It is a fold over a bag with `+`, so every part is
#   counted as many times as it appears, which is what a sum owes: a layer reached by two parts is
#   in the sum twice, and the elimination is what takes the second one back out.
#
# Readers compose this, not the parts
#   eliminations/paired.sqlc reads it to decide whether an elimination is wider than the sum it is
#   taken from, and composition/fused.sqlc to compare the sum with the figure the composer filed.
#   Each then meets it one row per key, so neither regroups what is summed here.
#   algebra/fusion_sum.sqlc recomputes the sum from its own route and stays independent of this.
-- composition/converted.sqlc folded to one row per composed layer and quantity.
SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer, c.quantity,
       sum(c.low)  AS sum_low,
       sum(c.mode) AS sum_mode,
       sum(c.high) AS sum_high,
       count(*)    AS parts
FROM (
    :compose(composition/converted.sqlc)
) c
GROUP BY c.composition, c.composed_layer, c.quantity
